Sir, I request that I will not be able to attend tomorrow;
because I am unwell.
In formal correspondence and complex sentence structures, the use of a comma after the address 'Sir' is considered standard. After the main clause, where the subordinate clause 'because' connects, a semicolon is used for more pause, and a full stop is used at the end of the sentence. Thus, this sentence is an excellent example of the correct and rule-compliant use of multiple punctuation marks.
